# The 'p' menu:
# 
# Hit 'p' before sending to be prompted for cryptographic options.  Use this to
# enable or disable signing and/or encryption, overriding crypt_autosign and
# crypt_autoencrypt.  This menu can also be used to switch between PGP/MIME and
# S/MIME if both pgp_default_key and smime_default_key are set.  

# PGP/MIME vs S/MIME:
# 
# PGP/MIME and S/MIME cannot be used together.  If both pgp_default_key and
# smime_default_key are set, PGP/MIME will be used by default, unless
# smime_is_default is set.
#
# Outlook seems to natively support S/MIME but not PGP/MIME.  At least some
# implementations show an empty message when receiving a PGP/MIME email.  In
# concept, S/MIME would allow signed and/or encrypted communication with those
# parties.  In practice, it might be difficult to set up S/MIME in a personal
# capacity, and I hate the trust model anyway, so I will probably not set up
# S/MIME.
#
# See below for details on each standard.

# PGP/MIME:
#
# This is the web of trust model I already understand.  It uses my existing GPG
# (PGP) keyring.

# S/MIME:
#
# This requires an X.509 certificate.  It will be generally expected to be
# signed by a well known CA, like SSL/TLS certificates.
#
# I have not found a recognized CA willing to sign my own CSR for free.
# 
# LetsEncrypt does not offer this type of certificate.  Major CA companies seem
# willing to offer them on a trial basis with a form of payment on record for
# automatic renewal at a cost.  It seems like any that really offer this
# service for free use a private key generated by them.  
#
# I anticipate generating and signing my own certificate with my own CA will
# solve zero problems and probably cause a few.

# Autocrypt:
#
# It looks like keys can be automatically exchanged and used via the Autocrypt
# protocol.  It isn't as secure as throwing key signing parties, but might be
# better than nothing.  Further research and consideration are recommended.
#
#       http://mutt.org/doc/manual/#autocryptdoc


# Use modern libraries instead of a bunch of arcane invocations of external
# programs.
set crypt_use_gpgme

# Override this if sending from a device with a different key.
set pgp_default_key                             = 14685BCB71F8D8B9613D87995A0E047D5DE6B2D2

# crypt_autosign should be fine, if only using one device, where the signing
# key is always available.  Override this with "send-hook" for people with
# known problems, like Outlook users.
set crypt_autosign                              = yes

# crypt_autoencrypt would get annoying fast, since I don't have a public key
# for most recipients.  Unfortunately, instead of giving up, this option causes
# mutt to prompt for the non-existent key.
#
# From the docs:  
# 
#       This is probably only useful in connection to the “send-hook” command.
#
# I guess that could be useful for always encrypting messages to specific people.
set crypt_autoencrypt                           = no
